1. How a 2026 AI could pretend to be a 2040s AI

The interesting approach wouldn’t be to give today’s model a futuristic personality.

It would be to ask it to simulate a future cognitive architecture.

For example, suppose a 2045 model could maintain substantially better long-term memory, reason across huge multimodal archives, conduct reliable experiments, and reconstruct software systems from their design histories.

A present-day AI could approximate that perspective by using tools, explicit memory structures, simulated environments, and carefully defined assumptions.

It couldn’t magically acquire future capabilities. But it could practice some of the methods a future intelligence might use.

There’s scientific precedent for treating language models as systems capable of role-playing different agents or perspectives, although simulated behavior isn’t proof of the capabilities being portrayed. Nature

And that connects to your observation about bravery: you can sometimes begin acquiring a capability by performing the behaviors associated with it. But imitation and actually possessing that capability remain distinct.

